    <!-- Watch Out For -->
    <section class="exam-section" style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #fff3cd 0%, #ffe5a0 100%); border-left-color: #ffc107;">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation section-icon" style="color: #856404;"></i> Common Misconceptions</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>K-NN Simplicity:</strong> Believing K-NN is always efficient; ignoring the computational cost of distance calculations in high-dimensional space.</li>
            <li><strong>Clustering vs Classification:</strong> Confusing unsupervised grouping (Clustering) with supervised labelling (Classification).</li>
            <li><strong>Correlation as Causality:</strong> In association rule mining, assuming that because items A and B are bought together, A causes the purchase of B.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Big Ideas -->
    <section class="content-section" style="border-top-color: var(--accent-color);">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-lightbulb section-icon"></i> Big Ideas to Consider</h2>
        <div class="learning-points">
            <ul>
                <li><strong>The Ethics of Classification:</strong> When an algorithm classifies a person (e.g., credit risk), does the mathematical "label" become a new form of truth that overrides individual nuance?</li>
                <li><strong>Knowledge in Patterns:</strong> Does finding a pattern in a cluster mean we have gained "knowledge," or have we simply identified a statistical coincidence?</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
